{
  "requirement": "No prompt, tool result, or tool error the harness gives the model MUST state a\nline number or line range of a project source, except within source text it\nquotes and where a tool error repeats a line number the model supplied. The\nharness MUST identify a source location by file and outline selector under\n{cite}`requirements.references.outline_selectors` instead.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The reviewed tests adequately check `requirements.authoring.no_line_numbers`. I split the requirement into three obligations: tool results, tool errors, and prompts. A fourth obligation is to identify locations by file and outline selector, which the tests also check.\n\n**Tool results.** Habeas read results, search results, outline results and reference results must not state line numbers.\n- `src/authoring/tools.rs#mod[tests]/fn[reading_records_only_delivered_sources_and_their_terms]` asserts that item reads and line-range reads carry only `item` and `kind`, with no `line_start` or `line_end`. It also asserts that recorded inputs are exactly the file, selector and hash.\n- `src/authoring/tools.rs#mod[tests]/fn[line_range_reads_record_the_innermost_covering_nodes]` asserts that line-range reads over Rust and plain-text files return selectors such as `fn[first]`, `mod[inner]/fn[second]` and `line@…`. It asserts there are no line fields, and that recorded inputs have no line fields or text.\n- `src/authoring/tools.rs#mod[tests]/fn[search_merges_context_windows_and_respects_ignore_rules]` and `src/authoring/tools.rs#mod[tests]/fn[separate_windows_in_one_node_are_divided_by_an_elision_line]` assert that ripgrep entries have no line fields. Each shown line is exactly `{text, matched}`, and separate windows use an elision line instead of numbers. The ast_grep tool shares `SearchNodes::entries` with ripgrep, so these tests cover its entries too.\n- `src/authoring/tools.rs#mod[tests]/fn[outline_lists_nested_nodes_without_text_or_inputs]` asserts that outline results equal exact JSON of selector, kind, name and children, and that plain-text entries lack `line_start`.\n- `src/authoring/tools.rs#mod[tests]/fn[references_list_names_and_flattened_sites_without_recording_inputs]` asserts that reference sites are exact `{name, role, file, item}` objects.\n\n**Tool errors.** Errors must not state parse-error or diagnostic lines, but may repeat a line number the model supplied.\n- `src/authoring/line_numbers.rs#mod[tests]/fn[tool_errors_drop_the_source_line_of_a_parse_error]` exercises `without_source_lines` on these forms:\n  - Rust parse errors of the form `line N: `.\n  - Query diagnostics of the form `file:N: `.\n  - A duplicate-declaration error that mentions a second location.\n  - YAML and TOML `at line N, column M` errors.\n  - The supplied-range repeats `file:9` and `file:3-9`, which must be preserved.\n- `src/authoring/mod.rs#mod[tests]/fn[tool_errors_give_the_model_no_line_numbers_it_did_not_supply]` is end to end. A read with `line_start: 99` repeats only the supplied number. A real parse error contains `line N: ` in the raw tool error, but the request the provider receives for the next turn contains \"cannot parse\" and no `line \\d+: `.\n\n**Prompts.**\n- `src/authoring/inputs.rs#mod[tests]/fn[anchored_files_contribute_root_node_inputs]` asserts exact JSON for the mandatory inputs. They are `{requirements, matches, text}` and `{file, item, hash, kind, text}` with no line fields. `model_mandatory_inputs` only adds issue states to these inputs.\n- `src/sites.rs#mod[tests]/fn[sites_are_the_innermost_nodes_of_site_matches_and_violations_within_them]` asserts that `matches_value` is exactly `{kind, query, item, text}`. It has no lines, and this is what the site prompt contains.\n- `src/authoring/mod.rs#mod[tests]/fn[site_reviews_use_their_prompt_and_record_applies_and_the_site]` asserts that the site subject message is the filled approved template, built from that value.\n- `src/authoring/mod.rs#mod[submission_description_tests]/fn[submission_descriptions_explain_arguments_and_successful_completion]` asserts that the submission tool descriptions require \"outline selectors\" and say \"never line numbers\".\n\n**Selectors instead of line numbers.** The assertions above on `item` values such as `fn[work]`, `mod[inner]/fn[second]`, `impl[Thing]/fn[run]` and `line@…` check the file-plus-selector alternative.\n\n**Gaps.** I found none. I assessed only the listed tests and did not search every prompt template for line numbers. Static prompt text has no conditional logic, and the dynamic parts (inputs, matches, errors) are all asserted above. No acknowledgments (`{fixme}`) apply.",
  "references": "5f07d59d3d70ca6a503110d2a797133c2b9f1eafd54fb53e7ee2fb02587ec51a",
